title: The MCP server
intro: Agents like Claude Code can drive the editor, and every feature reaches them by itself.
open: C:\Users\srako\Desktop\Projects\Griff Games\Hollowmere
scene: Lantern Road

chapter: An editor agents can drive | Help > MCP Server
say: While the editor is open, Griff now runs a Model Context Protocol server on this computer. Agents like Claude Code connect to it and use the editor much as a person does.
explain: menu:Help | The Help menu | MCP Server turns it on or off, and Copy MCP Setup Command copies the one line that adds it to Claude Code.
say: The server keeps no list of features. Every menu command is a tool, every component and script can be added and set, every question the editor asks can be answered, and every window and panel can be read and worked. A feature added tomorrow reaches agents the moment it exists.

chapter: New commands, new tools | File > Set as Start Scene
say: Two things that only lived in the Assets panel's right click menu are now commands: Open Scene, and Set as Start Scene.
explain: menu:File | The File menu | Being commands, they're in the File menu with shortcuts, and agents got them as tools, with nobody writing one.
menu: File > Set as Start Scene
say: Now this chapter is the one the game starts in.

chapter: Built through it | Hollowmere, chapter one
say: To test the server, an agent built this whole level through it: the tiles and art, the sounds and music, the lights, creatures, puzzles, menus and scripts, each placed with a tool call, then playtested by pressing keys in the running game.
play
wait: 2
hold: Right 1.2
tap: Space
hold: Right 1
say: Embers to gather, a lantern that lights the dark, and three zones, each with its own weather, light and music.
hold: Right 1.2
stop
